        "content": "1\t# talaria — Design Document\n2\t\n3\t*Status: draft v0.4 · amended 2026-08-03 · implementation in progress on `ralph/design`*\n4\t\n5\t> **Name resolved: `talaria`** — the winged sandals of Hermes. The tool is not the messenger;\n6\t> the agent is. This is what it wears to move fast. Fixes `cmd/talaria`, the binary on `$PATH`,\n7\t> `TALARIA_AUTH_*` env vars, and the `\"schema\": \"talaria/v1\"` output field.\n8\t\n9\t**Changes in v0.4:** three invariants the code needed and the doc never stated — credentials\n10\tbind to the spec's `servers[]` (§5a), a history entry is untrusted input when read (§5a), and a\n11\tsecurity scheme v1 cannot resolve is reported rather than silently ignored (§5). Each was found\n12\tby review finding the same defect in a new place; rationale in\n13\t[docs/design/proposals/2026-08-03-amendments.md](proposals/2026-08-03-amendments.md).\n14\t\n15\t**Changes in v0.3:** repositioned around a single idea — *an API client you can hand to an\n16\tagent*. The credential firewall moves from a design principle to the product's reason to exist\n17\t(§1, §2, §5a). Postman / Insomnia / Bruno enter the landscape as the actual incumbents (§2).\n18\t`history` added, unifying \"what did I call\" with the twin's corpus (§4, §5, §7). Name settled.\n19\t\n20\t---\n21\t\n22\t## 1. Vision\n23\t\n24\t**Postman and Insomnia for agents.**\n25\t\n26\tPoint it at any API's OpenAPI/Swagger doc and an agent can explore, call, and test that API —\n27\t**without ever being given your credentials.**\n28\t\n29\tThat second clause is the product. Every existing API client — Postman, Insomnia, Bruno, curl\n30\titself — assumes the operator is a human who is entitled to see their own secrets. Hand an\n31\tagent a Postman collection with an environment, or a `.bru` file, or a shell with\n32\t`$STRIPE_KEY` exported, and the agent has your key. It is in the context window, in the\n33\ttranscript, in the logs, and on whatever server processed the request.\n34\t\n35\tThis tool is the trust boundary. The agent operates on credential *names*; the binary resolves\n36\t*values* at the last possible moment and never emits them. See §5a — it is the core of the\n37\tdesign, not a hardening pass.\n38\t\n39\tThree capabilities, from one spec, in one binary:\n40\t\n41\t1. **An explorable API client** — list, describe, search, and call operations, curl underneath.\n42\t2. **A spec-driven tester** — validate real responses against the contract; smoke-test whole APIs.\n43\t3. **A digital twin** — a local server that mimics the real API for safe, fast, offline testing.\n44\t\n45\t**One-line pitch:** *Postman for agents. Point it at any API doc; your agent works the API\n46\tand never sees your credentials.*\n47\t\n48\t### Why an agent needs a different client\n49\t\n50\tPostman's value to a human is persistence and organisation: saved requests, curated\n51\tcollections, shared workspaces. An agent needs none of that — it re-derives what it needs from\n52\tthe spec on every run. What an agent needs instead, and what no existing client provides:\n53\t\n54\t| Agent need | Why existing clients fail it |\n55\t|---|---|\n56\t| **Never see credentials** | Every client assumes a human operator who owns the secrets |\n57\t| **Progressive discovery** | A collection or spec must be loaded whole; a 2MB `swagger.json` is a context bomb |\n58\t| **No setup step** | Collections must be built and maintained before they are useful |\n59\t| **Deterministic, parseable results** | GUI-first tools bolt on CLI runners as an afterthought |\n60\t| **Safety rails in the tool, not the prompt** | Nothing stops a collection runner from issuing `DELETE /users` |\n61\t\n62\t## 2. Why build this (landscape)\n63\t\n64\t### The incumbents under this framing\n65\t\n66\t| Tool | What it is | Why it does not serve an agent |\n67\t|---|---|---|\n68\t| **Postman** | The category default. GUI-first, cloud workspaces, `newman` CLI runner | Collection-centric; agent sees environment secrets; newman is a separate npm package on its own release cadence |\n69\t| **Insomnia** | GUI client with `inso` CLI, git-native storage | Same credential exposure; CLI is secondary to the GUI |\n70\t| **Bruno** | **The one that matters.** Git-native, offline-only, collections as plain `.bru` files, no account, has a CLI runner. **Crossed 44,000 GitHub stars in May 2026**, growth accelerated by Postman's March 2026 free-plan cut | Closest in spirit — local, plain-text, scriptable — but still collection-centric, and its `.bru` environments hand secrets straight to whoever runs them |\n71\t| **Hurl** | Plain-text `.hurl` files, assertions, git-native, CI-focused | A test runner, not an explorer; no spec awareness; secrets are plain in files or env |\n72\t| **Apidog** | Commercial all-in-one; **its CLI already emits structured JSON with `agentHints.nextSteps`** | Someone is already aiming at agent-readable output — but the credential model is unchanged |\n73\t\n74\t**Two things separate this tool from all of them:**\n75\t\n76\t1. **Spec-centric, not collection-centric.** They make you build and maintain a collection,\n77\t   which then drifts from the API. Here the OpenAPI doc *is* the collection. Nothing to curate,\n78\t   nothing to drift, and it works against an API the agent has never seen before — no setup step.\n79\t2. **The credential firewall.** They are built for a human who is entitled to the secrets.\n80\t   This is built for an operator who is not.\n81\t\n82\t### The adjacent camps\n83\t\n84\t| Camp | Examples | What they lack |\n85\t|---|---|---|\n86\t| Spec-aware CLI clients | **Restish** (Go, 1,348★, active) | No response validation — `--rsh-validate` is opt-in and covers *outbound JSON request bodies only*; it explicitly \"trusts explicit input and lets the server validate API semantics.\" No twin. No credential firewall. Its v2 is adding **MCP-ready extension points** — moving toward MCP as this moves away |\n87\t| Spec exploration for LLMs | **phyllotaxis** (Rust) | Read-only: no execution, validation, testing, or mocking. Overlaps Phase 1 only |\n88\t| Spec-based testers | **Schemathesis**, Dredd | CI tools, not interactive exploration; Python/Node runtimes |\n89\t| OpenAPI→MCP bridges | FastMCP, AWS openapi-mcp-server, Speakeasy, Stainless | Context bloat (below); a running server per API |\n90\t| **Code execution / Code Mode** | Cloudflare Code Mode, Agentgateway, Anthropic's code-execution pattern | The most serious architectural rival. Requires a code sandbox; no response-vs-spec validation; no twin; leaves no artifact a human can paste into a terminal |\n91\t| Mock servers | Prism, **Specmatic**, Microcks, **WireMock**, Mockoon | Standalone; no integrated exploration/testing CLI; no agent ergonomics |\n92\t| curl generators | curlgenerator, Swama | No execution, validation, or testing |\n93\t\n94\t### The token problem is real and now mainstream\n95\t\n96\t- **Cloudflare's API has 2,500+ endpoints.** As an MCP server that is **1.17M+ tokens**; their\n97\t  Code Mode (Feb 2026) exposes the same API in ~1,000 tokens via two tools.\n98\t- **Anthropic's code-execution pattern** measured 150,000 → 2,000 tokens on one workflow — 98.7%.\n99\t- **Perplexity's CTO** announced an internal shift away from MCP over context waste and auth friction.\n100\t\n101\t**Counterweight, stated honestly:** MCP has crossed 8M server downloads and 97M monthly SDK\n102\tdownloads, with Google, OpenAI and Microsoft shipping it. \"Not an MCP server, ever\" (§9) is a\n103\tproduct *stance*, not a prediction that MCP loses.\n104\t\n105\t### Honest competitive assessment\n106\t\n107\t- **Bruno is the incumbent to beat on developer sentiment**, and it is winning that market on\n108\t  git-nativeness and price, not on agent ergonomics. It is not trying to solve the credential\n109\t  problem. That is the opening.\n110\t- **Restish is the closest technical competitor** and it is actively developed. Do not claim it\n111\t  has no validation; claim it has no *response* validation, no twin, and no credential firewall.\n112\t- **phyllotaxis proves the exploration idea by independent invention** — read-only, small, and\n113\t  worth reading before designing `describe` (§8).\n114\t- **The twin's mechanism is not novel.** Specmatic already records real traffic into\n115\t  spec-validated mocks; WireMock has record & playback plus stateful create-then-fetch.\n116\t  Replay and statefulness are **table stakes** — see §6 for what actually differentiates.\n117\t- **Code Mode is the most serious architectural rival**, backed by Cloudflare and Anthropic.\n118\t  The honest edge: it needs a code sandbox and produces nothing a human can paste into a\n119\t  terminal; this needs only a shell and emits a curl command every time.\n120\t\n121\tPrimary motivation remains: a tool for my own workflow. Open source is upside.\n122\t\n123\t## 3. Design principles\n124\t\n125\t0. **Secrets never reach the agent. Priority one, non-negotiable.**\n126\t   This is §1 — the reason the tool exists — not a hardening measure. The binary is the trust\n127\t   boundary: the agent operates entirely on credential *names* (env vars, profile names);\n128\t   credential *values* are injected at the last possible moment and never appear on stdout,\n129\t   stderr, in dry-runs, in emitted curl commands, in error messages, in history, or in\n130\t   recordings. See §5a for the threat model. Any feature that would print a secret is a bug,\n131\t   even if the user asked for it — **there is no `--show-secrets` flag.**\n132\t\n133\t1. **Agent-friendly = good CLI design, enforced strictly.**\n134\t   - Machine-readable output everywhere: `--output json` on every command.\n135\t     Default: pretty when stdout is a TTY, JSON when piped.\n136\t   - Deterministic exit codes (§4). Agents branch on these.\n137\t   - Never prompt. Never page. No interactivity, ever. Dangerous behaviour is gated by flags\n138\t     set deliberately by a human, not by confirmations an agent can answer itself.\n139\t   - Errors to stderr as structured JSON: what failed, why, valid alternatives.\n140\t   - Versioned output schema (`\"schema\": \"talaria/v1\"`) so agent prompts don't break.\n141\t\n142\t2. **Progressive disclosure is the product.** `list` is one compact line per operation;\n143\t   `describe` renders schemas in a terse readable form (`name*: (string) description`), never\n144\t   raw JSON Schema dumps. Neither human nor agent ever loads the raw spec into context.\n145\t\n146\t3. **No collection to maintain.** The spec is the collection. There is no import step, no\n147\t   curation, no drift. A spec URL and a credential name is the entire setup.\n148\t\n149\t4. **curl is the execution engine and the lingua franca.** Build argv via `os/exec` (never\n150\t   shell strings — no quoting hell, no injection). `--dry-run` prints the exact curl command.\n151\t   Every executed call returns its curl equivalent: a portable reproduction for bug reports,\n152\t   docs, and scripts. **Emitted curl always references secrets symbolically** — e.g.\n153\t   `-H \"Authorization: Bearer $TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER\"` — runnable in a shell where the env var is\n154\t   set, useless to exfiltrate (§5a). **Check curl's version, not just presence:**\n155\t   `--write-out '%{json}'` requires curl ≥ 7.70 and is a hard floor.\n156\t\n157\t5. **Safe by default.** Read-only (GET/HEAD/OPTIONS) unless `--allow-mutations`. The sanctioned\n158\t   answer to \"I need to test a DELETE\" is: do it on the twin. Recordings redact by default.\n159\t\n160\t6. **Composability over features.** Plain JSON to stdout for jq; no built-in query language in\n161\t   v1. Spec via arg, `--spec`, or env var. **The process owns its own stdin** (§5a) — `--body -`\n162\t   works and never competes with how secrets reach curl.\n163\t\n164\t7. **The agent README is a first-class deliverable.** An operating manual written for LLMs: the\n165\t   list→describe→dry-run→call workflow, exit codes, output shapes, and — most importantly — how\n166\t   to name a credential you cannot see. Ships as `AGENT.md`; doubles as a pi skill and a Claude\n167\t   Code skill wrapping the same binary. Treat it with the same care as code.\n168\t\n169\t## 4. CLI surface\n170\t\n171\t```\n172\t# Discovery (cheap, no network beyond fetching the spec)\n173\ttalaria list [spec] [--tag t] [--output json|pretty|tsv]\n174\ttalaria describe [spec] <operationId> [--output json|pretty]\n175\ttalaria search [spec] <query> [--kind operation|schema|param]   # fuzzy find across the spec\n176\ttalaria uses [spec] <schemaName>                                # reverse lookup: which operations use it\n177\t\n178\t# Calling\n179\ttalaria call [spec] <operationId>\n180\t    --param id=42                 # path params\n181\t    --query verbose=true          # query params (repeatable)\n182\t    --header X-Foo=bar            # extra headers (repeatable)\n183\t    --body '{\"...\"}' | --body @file.json | --body -   # stdin\n184\t    --base-url https://staging.example.com\n185\t    --profile staging             # named config: base-url + auth + headers\n186\t    --dry-run                     # print curl command, send nothing, exit 0\n187\t    --allow-mutations             # required for POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE\n188\t    --timeout 30                  # seconds; give up on the request rather than hang\n189\t    --output json|pretty\n190\t\n191\t# History — what did I call, what came back\n192\ttalaria history [--operation id] [--since 1h] [--status 4xx] [--output json]\n193\ttalaria history show <n>           # full request/response, redacted\n194\ttalaria history replay <n>         # re-issue a past call\n195\t\n196\t# Smoke testing\n197\ttalaria run [spec] [--tag t] [--operation id ...]\n198\t    --base-url ... --profile ...\n199\t    --allow-mutations\n200\t    --report json|junit|pretty\n201\t    --timeout 30                  # seconds, per operation; one wedged endpoint never costs the report\n202\t    --fail-on-error               # nonzero exit if any HTTP >= 400\n203\t\n204\t# Digital twin (later phases)\n205\ttalaria twin serve [spec] --port 9000 [--corpus ./twin-data] [--stateful]\n206\ttalaria twin record [spec] --upstream https://api.real.com --port 9000 --corpus ./twin-data\n207\ttalaria twin fault <operationId> --status 429 --rate 0.1 [--header Retry-After=30]\n208\t\n209\t# Meta\n210\ttalaria auth check [spec] [--profile p]   # are credentials PRESENT for the spec's security\n211\t                                         # schemes? never prints values:\n212\t                                         # {\"scheme\":\"bearerAuth\",\"source\":\"env:TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER\",\"present\":true}\n213\ttalaria version\n214\t```\n215\t\n216\t**`search` and `uses`** are adopted from phyllotaxis. They matter because an agent usually knows\n217\ta *domain concept* (\"something about invoices\") or a *data model* (\"what touches `Invoice`?\")\n218\trather than an operationId — and `list` on a large spec is the context dump this tool exists to\n219\tavoid.\n220\t\n221\t**`history` is new in v0.3**, and it is the one genuine feature the Postman framing adds. It\n222\tanswers \"what have I already tried and what came back\", which is the question an agent asks\n223\tconstantly and currently cannot. It costs little because **history and the twin's corpus are the\n224\tsame data** — every call already produces a redacted request/response pair. Local history is the\n225\tseed; the recording proxy (Phase 5) is the industrial version. `history replay` also gives the\n226\tuseful half of request chaining without the scenario DSL (§8).\n227\t\n228\tSpec argument accepts a file path or URL (with local cache); falls back to an env var.\n229\tSupports Swagger 2.0 and OpenAPI 3.0/3.1/3.2, JSON and YAML (§5).\n230\t\n231\t### Output shape for `call` (sketch)\n232\t\n233\t```json\n234\t{\n235\t  \"schema\": \"talaria/v1\",\n236\t  \"request\": {\n237\t    \"curl\": \"curl -q -s -H \\\"Authorization: Bearer $TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER\\\" 'https://…'\",\n238\t    \"method\": \"GET\", \"url\": \"…\",\n239\t    \"headers\": { \"Authorization\": \"<redacted:env:TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER>\" }\n240\t  },\n241\t  \"response\": { \"status\": 200, \"headers\": {}, \"body\": {}, \"timing_ms\": 143 },\n242\t  \"validation\": { \"status_documented\": true, \"body_valid\": true, \"errors\": [] }\n243\t}\n244\t```\n245\t\n246\t### Exit codes\n247\t\n248\t| Code | Meaning |\n249\t|---|---|\n250\t| 0 | Success (HTTP 4xx/5xx is exit 0 for `call` — a 404 is a successful *observation*; use `--fail-on-error` to change) |\n251\t| 1 | Request could not be completed (network, curl failure) |\n252\t| 2 | Usage error (unknown operation, missing required param — stderr JSON lists valid options) |\n253\t| 3 | Spec parse/load error |\n254\t| 4 | Validation failure (response violates spec) — only with `--fail-on-error` / `run` |\n255\t| 5 | Credential missing for a required security scheme (distinct from usage error so agents can act on it: *ask the human to set `$NAME`*) |\n256\t\n257\t## 5. Architecture\n258\t\n259\t```\n260\tcmd/talaria/main.go         # cobra wiring\n261\tinternal/spec/             # load (file/URL/cache), v2→v3 convert, normalize\n262\tinternal/operation/        # THE core model: id, method, path, params, body schema,\n263\t                           #   auth requirements, response contracts\n264\tinternal/secret/           # SecretRef, resolution, redaction  ← the trust boundary\n265\tinternal/curl/             # argv + config-document builder, executor (os/exec)\n266\tinternal/validate/         # request & response vs schema  ← shared with twin\n267\tinternal/gen/              # example/schema-based data generation ← shared with twin\n268\tinternal/corpus/           # history + recordings: store, redact, index, generalize\n269\tinternal/twin/             # http server: replay, fallback gen, state, faults\n270\tinternal/output/           # json / pretty / tsv renderers, versioned schemas\n271\tinternal/config/           # profiles, env vars, auth mapping\n272\t```\n273\t\n274\tKey boundary rules:\n275\t- `operation`, `validate`, `gen` are consumed by **both** the curl executor and the twin server.\n276\t  Nothing in them may import `curl` or `twin`.\n277\t- `secret` is imported by nearly everything but **resolves** values for exactly one caller:\n278\t  the `curl` executor at exec time (§5a).\n279\t- `corpus` backs both `history` and the twin, so it is defined in Phase 2 even though the\n280\t  recording proxy lands in Phase 5.\n281\t- Twin's serve side uses `net/http` directly — curl is only for outbound calls.\n282\t\n283\t### Library choices\n284\t\n285\t- **CLI:** spf13/cobra (+ viper for profile/env layering). Cobra's generated help is\n286\t  well-structured text that agents read happily.\n287\t- **OpenAPI 3.x:** `pb33f/libopenapi` — a 3.2/3.1/3.0 + Overlays + Arazzo toolkit, actively\n288\t  developed, and what Restish migrated to.\n289\t- **Swagger 2.0:** `getkin/kin-openapi`'s **`openapi2conv`**, used *only* to convert 2.0 → 3.x\n290\t  at load time. libopenapi cannot do this: its v2 model is unmaintained, slated for removal,\n291\t  and its authors say \"**DO NOT** take a dependency on it\" and \"we don't recommend using it…\n292\t  it's a commercial product.\" Two OpenAPI dependencies is the cost of the \"any API doc\"\n293\t  promise — much of the real world is still 2.0. Conversion runs once at load, so everything\n294\t  downstream sees one model. **Phase 1 must verify conversion fidelity against real 2.0 specs.**\n295\t- **Schema & message validation:** `pb33f/libopenapi-validator`. Validates `http.Request` *and*\n296\t  `http.Response` against a 3.x spec and ships a standalone OpenAPI router with auth callbacks\n297\t  and replayable bodies — most of what the twin needs, from the spec model the CLI already\n298\t  holds. Watch one behaviour: it defaults to OpenAPI 3.1+ strict JSON Schema semantics, which\n299\t  can be stricter than a 3.0 spec intends; verify against 3.0 specs early.\n300\t- **Execution:** system curl via `os/exec`. Not a Go HTTP client — the curl command *is* a\n301\t  feature (transparency, reproducibility).\n302\t\n303\t### Auth\n304\t\n305\tThe spec declares security schemes; credentials come from outside and are named, never shown:\n306\t- Env vars by convention: `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER`, `TALARIA_AUTH_BASIC`, `TALARIA_AUTH_APIKEY_<SCHEME>`.\n307\t- Profiles (`~/.config/talaria/config.yaml`, mode 0600): named sets of base-url + auth +\n308\t  headers, selected with `--profile`. Secrets in profiles may reference env vars.\n309\t- `auth check` reports which schemes are satisfied and from which *source* — never the value —\n310\t  so an agent can diagnose a broken auth setup blind and tell the human exactly what to set.\n311\t- v1 resolves `http bearer`, `http basic`, and `apiKey` (header, query, cookie) schemes.\n312\t- A scheme outside that set — `oauth2`, `openIdConnect`, `mutualTLS` — is **unsupported, not\n313\t  invisible**. When an operation requires one:\n314\t  - if `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER` is set, the scheme is satisfied by that token. This *is* \"bring\n315\t    your own token\": the user obtained it however the flow demands, and talaria carries it;\n316\t  - if it is not set, the operation is unsatisfiable. `auth check` reports\n317\t    `{\"scheme\":\"oauth2\",\"supported\":false,\"present\":false}` and exits 5; `call` and `run` exit 5\n318\t    with a structured error naming the scheme and the variable to set.\n319\t- **`auth check` never reports a scheme satisfied when the call would refuse it.** The two agree\n320\t  by construction, or `auth check` is worthless to an agent. Implementing OAuth *flows* remains\n321\t  out of scope; Restish shows the cost of doing them properly.\n322\t\n323\t## 5a. The credential firewall\n324\t\n325\t**Invariant: no consumer of stdout or stderr ever observes a credential value.**\n326\t\n327\tThe setup is asymmetric by design. A human (or CI) places secrets in env vars or a profile file\n328\tonce. The agent only ever names them. This is what makes it safe to point an agent at a\n329\tproduction API doc.\n330\t\n331\t### How secrets reach curl: the config document on curl's stdin\n332\t\n333\t**Primary mechanism: `curl -K -`.** curl reads a config document from stdin when the filename\n334\tis `-`. Secret-bearing headers, credentials, and the request body go in that document;\n335\t**nothing sensitive is ever an argv element.** This is the documented way to keep credentials\n336\tout of the process list — `/proc/*/cmdline` is readable by any process on the host, including\n337\tones the agent spawns.\n338\t\n339\t**Resolving stdin contention.** `--body -` (body from stdin) and `-K -` (config from stdin)\n340\tappear to collide. They do not:\n341\t\n342\t> **The Go process owns the real stdin. curl's stdin is always a fresh pipe that Go writes.**\n343\t\n344\t`--body -` is read to EOF by the Go process from *its* stdin. curl is then spawned with a new\n345\tpipe on *its* stdin, carrying the config document — including the body as a `data` directive.\n346\tOne stdin consumer at each level. For large or binary bodies, fall back to a 0600 temp file\n347\treferenced as `data = \"@/path\"`, deleted immediately after exec. The temp file is the exception.\n348\t\n349\t### Credentials bind to hosts\n350\t\n351\t**Invariant: a resolved credential is transmitted only to a host the spec declares, or one a\n352\thuman has explicitly allowed.**\n353\t\n354\tRedaction answers *does the secret appear in output*. It does not answer *which host may\n355\treceive it* — and without that second rule, `--base-url https://attacker.example` sends a\n356\tproduction key to an attacker while violating nothing.\n357\t\n358\tThe allowed host set for a call is:\n359\t\n360\t1. every host in the spec's `servers[]`, after server-variable substitution; plus\n361\t2. every host passed as `--allow-host HOST` (repeatable); plus\n362\t3. every host in `allow_hosts:` in the active profile.\n363\t\n364\tWhen `--base-url` points outside that set the request still runs, but **every credential is\n365\twithheld**, and the omission is reported both ways — a one-line stderr warning naming the\n366\twithheld schemes and the offending host, and a machine-readable field in the envelope so an\n367\tagent can act on it instead of inferring it from a downstream 401:\n368\t\n369\t```json\n370\t\"credentials_withheld\": [\n371\t  {\"scheme\": \"bearerAuth\", \"reason\": \"host not in spec servers[]\", \"host\": \"localhost:9000\"}\n372\t]\n373\t```\n374\t\n375\tWithholding rather than refusing is deliberate: pointing at a local twin is the most common\n376\t`--base-url` use, and the twin accepts placeholder credentials by design (§6). An agent working\n377\tagainst the twin must not need a flag, and must not be handed a real secret.\n378\t\n379\t`auth check` reports against the *resolved* host set, so \"present\" never means \"will actually\n380\tbe sent\".\n381\t\n382\tThis is default-deny with a deliberate override, the same shape as `--allow-mutations` in §3.5.\n383\t\n384\t### History is untrusted input when read\n385\t\n386\tRedaction at write time protects what *leaves* the tool. It says nothing about what comes back\n387\t*in*. A history file is a persistent artifact: it may have been written by another project,\n388\tcopied from another machine, or edited by hand. **Every field in a history entry is untrusted\n389\tinput.**\n390\t\n391\t`history replay` therefore re-derives rather than replays:\n392\t\n393\t| Field | Treatment on replay |\n394\t|---|---|\n395\t| operationId, params, body | Re-bound through the normal request-construction path, re-validated against the current spec |\n396\t| Credentials | **Never taken from the entry.** Re-resolved from the current environment and profile, subject to the host-binding rule above |\n397\t| Target host | From the current `--base-url`, profile, or spec — never from the stored URL. If the stored host is outside the currently allowed set, replay refuses with exit 2 rather than silently retargeting |\n398\t| Env var names | Only names inside the `TALARIA_AUTH_*` namespace are resolvable. A stored entry naming any other variable is malformed, not a lookup |\n399\t| Sizes and types | Bounded and type-checked before use. A corrupt or hostile entry fails that entry, never the process |\n400\t\n401\tThe rule in one line: **a history entry is data, never instruction.**\n402\t\n403\t### Leak channels and countermeasures\n404\t\n405\t| Leak channel | Countermeasure |\n406\t|---|---|\n407\t| Emitted/dry-run curl commands | Always symbolic: `-H \"Authorization: Bearer $TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER\"`. Runnable where the env var exists; useless to exfiltrate |\n408\t| `request.headers` in JSON output | Sensitive headers rendered `<redacted:env:NAME>`. Built-in list (`Authorization`, `Cookie`, `Proxy-Authorization`, `*api*key*`, `*token*`, `*secret*`) + user-extensible. Applies to pretty output too |\n409\t| curl process argv (`ps`, `/proc/*/cmdline`) | Never argv. Config document on curl's stdin (above) |\n410\t| Response headers/bodies (`Set-Cookie`, login endpoints returning `access_token`) | Redact `Set-Cookie` and configurable response-body JSON paths by default; document that auth-issuing endpoints are for humans, not agents |\n411\t| **History** | Same redaction as output, applied at write time. History is a permanent artifact — it is the highest-risk surface in the tool |\n412\t| Error paths (curl stderr, validation errors quoting the request) | Errors are built from the redacted representation, never the raw one. Test explicitly — error paths are where redaction bugs live |\n413\t| Recordings / corpus | Redaction at **write** time, not read time. Un-redacted recording is not an option |\n414\t| Spec cache, verbose/debug logs | Debug output goes through the same redacted representation. No `--verbose` that bypasses it |\n415\t| Query-string API keys (`?api_key=`) | Symbolic in emitted curl, `<redacted>` in output URL fields. These still leak into *server* logs — warn once on stderr |\n416\t\n417\t**Architecture consequence.** Exactly one component (`internal/curl`, at exec time) ever holds\n418\tresolved secret values, and only long enough to write the config document. Everything else —\n419\toutput, validation, history, corpus, errors — operates on a `Request` whose secret fields are\n420\t*structurally* references (`SecretRef{EnvVar: \"…\"}`), not strings. Leaking then requires\n421\tdeliberately resolving a ref, which is greppable and reviewable. Forgetting to scrub a string\n422\tis neither.\n423\t\n424\t**Twin synergy.** The twin needs **zero real credentials**. It enforces that *a* credential is\n425\tpresent (auth realism) but accepts placeholders — so the agent's entire develop-and-test loop\n426\truns secret-free, and real credentials exist only in a final human- or CI-gated run.\n427\t\n428\t**Testing requirement.** A CI suite injects canary secrets through every auth mechanism and\n429\tgreps every output surface — json, pretty, tsv, dry-run, errors, junit reports, history store,\n430\tcorpus files, debug logs — for them. Redaction regressions fail the build. **This suite is\n431\tPhase 2 work and gates every release thereafter.**\n432\t\n433\t### Threats explicitly not covered\n434\t\n435\tHonesty about the boundary's limits:\n436\t- **A response body containing a secret** is returned to the agent, because the tool cannot know\n437\t  a field is sensitive unless configured. Mitigated by default `Set-Cookie` redaction and\n438\t  configurable body paths; not solved.\n439\t- **An agent that can read env vars or the profile file directly** bypasses everything here.\n440\t  This tool secures the *API-calling* path, not the machine. Running the agent without those\n441\t  vars in its environment — and letting the binary read them from a file it alone opens — is\n442\t  the deployment that fully realises the boundary.\n443\t- **Server-side logs** may record query-string keys regardless of what this tool does.\n444\t\n445\t### Test data for `run` mode\n446\t\n447\tPriority order: spec `example`/`examples` → user fixture files (`--fixtures dir/`, matched by\n448\toperationId) → schema-generated data (`gen`). Examples-first keeps requests realistic.\n449\t\n450\t## 6. The digital twin\n451\t\n452\t**Goal: mimic the real API, not just return schema-valid noise.**\n453\t\n454\t**What is table stakes and what is not.** Replay-from-recordings and stateful CRUD already exist\n455\tin mature products — Specmatic records real traffic into spec-validated mocks; WireMock does\n456\trecord & playback plus stateful create-then-fetch. Building those buys parity, not advantage.\n457\tThree things are genuinely differentiated:\n458\t\n459\t1. **Synthesis by generalization from the corpus** — synthesize user 99 by mutating recorded\n460\t   users 42 and 57: real field values, real enum usage, real nullability, instead of\n461\t   `\"string\"`/`0` schema noise.\n462\t2. **Deterministic fault injection from the same CLI that explores and tests** — no second\n463\t   tool, no second config format, no glue.\n464\t3. **Zero-credential operation** — the develop-and-test loop runs without a real secret ever\n465\t   existing on the machine (§5a). This is the twin's tightest link to §1.\n466\t\n467\t| Dimension | Mechanism |\n468\t|---|---|\n469\t| **Data realism** | Recordings over generation; generalize from them (1 above) |\n470\t| **Behavioral realism (state)** | Infer CRUD lifecycles from REST conventions + spec (POST returns created object with id; path param matches id field). In-memory/bbolt store so create→get→delete coheres. Recorded sequences confirm inferred semantics |\n471\t| **Error realism** | Injectable faults via CLI or a `POST /_twin/faults` control endpoint. Real error bodies harvested from recordings. Deterministic faults are where the twin beats the real API |\n472\t| **Temporal realism** | Per-endpoint latency profiles from recorded timings (already captured via curl `--write-out`). Optional rate-limit simulation. Nice-to-have |\n473\t| **Auth realism** | Enforce the spec's security schemes; reject missing/bad credentials with the status codes seen in recordings. Accepts placeholder tokens |\n474\t\n475\t**Recording is the spine.** The spec provides structure; recorded traffic provides truth. Every\n476\ttwin feature consumes the same corpus — the same store that backs `history`. Day 1 is a\n477\tspec-only static mock; explore the real API through the recording proxy and the twin gets\n478\tprogressively more real, automatically. *\"Your twin gets better the more you use the real API.\"*\n479\t\n480\t**Request validation on the twin.** It validates incoming requests against the spec and returns\n481\tprecise schema errors, doubling as a client-side contract checker. `libopenapi-validator`'s\n482\trouter and request validator provide this directly.\n483\t\n484\t**The loop this unlocks** (identical commands, swap `--base-url`):\n485\t\n486\t```\n487\ttalaria twin record spec.yaml --upstream https://api.real.com --corpus ./twin\n488\ttalaria call spec.yaml listUsers --base-url http://localhost:9000        # via proxy, recorded\n489\ttalaria twin serve spec.yaml --corpus ./twin --stateful\n490\ttalaria run spec.yaml --base-url http://localhost:9000 --allow-mutations # safe destructive testing\n491\ttalaria run spec.yaml --profile staging                                   # final verification\n492\t```\n493\t\n494\tThe agent explores the real API read-only with recording on, builds and tests destructive flows\n495\tagainst the twin while injecting faults, then runs an identical smoke test against staging. It\n496\tnever needs mutation rights on a shared environment for 95% of its work.\n497\t\n498\t**Security requirement (non-negotiable, ships with the recording proxy):** recordings contain\n499\treal data. Redact `Authorization`, `Cookie`, `Set-Cookie`, and `*key*`/`*token*` headers by\n500\tdefault; support a redaction config before any cassette is written. Document \"don't commit\n501\tunredacted corpora.\"\n502\t\n503\t**Known-hard territory (be honest in docs):** state inference is heuristic and breaks on\n504\tnon-CRUD APIs, cross-resource side effects, and server-computed fields. Ship it explicitly\n505\tbest-effort with per-resource overrides, after the recording tiers prove out.\n506\t\n507\t## 7. Roadmap\n508\t\n509\tEach phase ships something independently useful. The differentiated, low-competition work\n510\tships before the twin, which is the hardest and competes with mature funded products for the\n511\tleast marginal advantage.\n512\t\n513\t| Phase | Deliverable | Notes |\n514\t|---|---|---|\n515\t| 1 | `list`, `describe`, `search`, `uses`, `call --dry-run` | Spec loading (3.x via libopenapi, 2.0 via `openapi2conv`), operation model, curl builder. Fully offline-testable. Already useful as a spec→curl tool. **Verify 2.0 conversion against real specs here** |\n516\t| 2 | Real execution + **the credential firewall** + `history` | JSON output, exit codes, `--base-url`, profiles, env-var auth, mutation gating, `AGENT.md` v1. **All of §5a ships here:** `SecretRef`, symbolic curl, redacted output, `-K -` config-on-stdin, stdin-ownership rule, `auth check`, exit code 5, canary CI suite. Plus the corpus store behind `history`. **Not retrofittable — it shapes the core `Request` type.** This phase is the product |\n517\t| 3 | Response validation | Status documented? Body matches schema? Content-type? `validation` block; exit code 4. Built on libopenapi-validator |\n518\t| 4 | `run` smoke mode | Tag/operation filters, examples→fixtures→gen data, JUnit/JSON reports. CI-ready. **End of the differentiated core — ship it, use it, and let real usage decide whether the twin earns its keep** |\n519\t| 5 | Recording proxy + redaction | The corpus spine, industrial version. Useful alone |\n520\t| 6 | `twin serve`: replay + spec fallback + request validation | Prism parity, corpus-fed. Router from libopenapi-validator |\n521\t| 7 | Stateful twin + data synthesis | CRUD inference, generalization — the actual differentiator (§6) |\n522\t| 8 | Fault & latency injection | Control endpoint + CLI; latency profiles from corpus |\n523\t\n524\t**Decision gate after Phase 4.** Phases 5–8 are roughly as much work as 1–4 and land in a\n525\tcrowded, well-funded market. Do not start them on faith; start them because using Phases 1–4\n526\tmade the absence of a twin painful.\n527\t\n528\tDistribution: single static binaries per platform (GitHub releases), `go install`, a curl-able\n529\tinstall script so agents can bootstrap the tool mid-session, Homebrew tap later. Packaged as a\n530\tpi package and a Claude Code skill wrapping the same binary.\n531\t\n532\t## 8. Open questions\n533\t\n534\t- ~~**Name.**~~ **Resolved: `talaria`** — Hermes's winged sandals. It names the *equipment*,\n535\t  not the messenger, which is the right relationship: the agent is the messenger, this is what\n536\t  it wears. Homebrew formula free; the only GitHub collision is `talariadb/talaria` (230★,\n537\t  quiet, a time-series store). Rejected after collision checks: `hermes` (NousResearch's\n538\t  hermes-agent, 224k★, same space — fatal), `iris` (kataras/iris, 25.5k★ Go web framework —\n539\t  fatal for a Go binary), `missive`, `legate`, `tambo`, `envoi`. Runners-up worth recording in\n540\t  case of a future pivot: `nuncio` (2★, cleanest namespace), `mochila` (the Pony Express\n541\t  mailbag, swapped horse-to-horse in under two minutes), `angareion` (the Persian relay\n542\t  Herodotus described in the passage that became \"neither snow nor rain…\").\n543\t- **Request chaining.** Out of scope for v1, but the Postman framing makes its absence\n544\t  conspicuous — chaining is the main reason people build collections. `history replay` (§4)\n545\t  covers the common case without a scenario DSL. Revisit only if real usage demands it;\n546\t  Schemathesis does this via OpenAPI `links` if a reference is needed.\n547\t- **History retention and location.** Where does it live (`~/.local/state/talaria/`? per-project\n548\t  `.talaria/`?), how much is kept, and is it opt-out? It is the highest-risk artifact in the\n549\t  tool (§5a) and needs a deliberate answer, not a default.\n550\t- **libopenapi-validator strictness on 3.0 specs.** ~~It defaults to 3.1+ strict JSON Schema\n551\t  behaviour. Confirm in Phase 3 whether that yields false failures on real 3.0 specs and\n552\t  whether it can be configured down per-document.~~ **Resolved, Phase 3 (v0.14.0):** no false\n553\t  failures, and no per-document configuration needed. The library reads the document's own\n554\t  OpenAPI version and, for 3.0, rewrites the draft-04-era constructs before compiling —\n555\t  `nullable: true` becomes a `\"null\"` union, boolean `exclusiveMinimum`/`exclusiveMaximum`\n556\t  become the numeric 2020-12 spelling, and singular `example` is ignored rather than rejected.\n557\t  `internal/validate` therefore passes no strictness options. Pinned by an adversarial 3.0\n558\t  fixture (`internal/validate/testdata/strict-3.0.yaml`) carrying all four constructs, so a\n559\t  library upgrade that re-imposed 3.1 semantics would fail the suite.\n560\t- **Default output.** Locked: pretty-on-TTY, JSON-when-piped — revisit if agents get confused\n561\t  by TTY detection in odd sandboxes (`--output` always wins).\n562\t- **`describe` compact schema format.** The agent-facing UX centrepiece; design it early and\n563\t  prototype against big real specs (GitHub, Stripe). phyllotaxis is prior art worth reading.\n564\t- **Body-in-config encoding.** The config document needs correct escaping for arbitrary JSON\n565\t  bodies (`data = \"…\"` with backslash escapes). Decide the cutover to the temp-file fallback\n566\t  (size? binary content-type?) in Phase 2.\n567\t- **Twin state overrides.** Config format for correcting bad CRUD inference (per-resource id\n568\t  field, collection path, relations).\n569\t\n570\t## 9. Non-goals\n571\t\n572\t- **Not a secrets manager.** It reads credentials from env vars and a profile file; it does not\n573\t  store, rotate, or broker them. Point it at whatever you already use.\n574\t- **Not a collection manager.** No saved requests, no workspaces, no sharing. The spec is the\n575\t  collection (§3.3). This is the deliberate break from Postman.\n576\t- Not a general HTTP client (that's curl/HTTPie/Restish).\n577\t- Not property-based fuzzing (that's Schemathesis; a future `fuzz` command is possible, but\n578\t  don't dilute v1).\n579\t- **Not an MCP server. Ever.** The absence is the point — see §2 for the honest framing.\n580\t- Not a Code Mode / SDK generator. Different bet, different tradeoffs (§2).\n581\t- No GraphQL, no gRPC. OpenAPI/Swagger REST only.\n582\t- No interactive TUI mode in v1.\n583\t"
      }
    ]
  },
  "parent_tool_use_id": null,
  "session_id": "092252dc-0844-4f15-b0df-e31249f7821e",
