        "tool_use_id": "toolu_01Yb12tof7AJDHxS2H8zj7KQ",
        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "1\t// Package spec loads OpenAPI descriptions into one in-memory representation.\n2\t// Everything downstream — the operation model, list, describe, call — reads a\n3\t// *Document and never touches the parser, so the differences between JSON and\n4\t// YAML, between 3.0 and 3.1, and (from Task 5) between Swagger 2.0 and OpenAPI\n5\t// 3.x stop at this package boundary.\n6\tpackage spec\n7\t\n8\timport (\n9\t\tv3high \"github.com/pb33f/libopenapi/datamodel/high/v3\"\n10\t)\n11\t\n12\t// Document is a loaded, built OpenAPI 3.x description. Model is the high-level\n13\t// libopenapi model with references already resolved; callers read it directly\n14\t// rather than re-parsing the source bytes.\n15\ttype Document struct {\n16\t\t// Version is the exact spec version string, e.g. \"3.0.3\" or \"3.1.0\".\n17\t\tVersion string\n18\t\t// Source describes where the bytes came from — a file path, a URL, or\n19\t\t// \"<bytes>\" for an in-memory spec. It exists so errors raised well after\n20\t\t// loading can still say which spec they came from.\n21\t\tSource string\n22\t\t// Model is the built OpenAPI 3.x model.\n23\t\tModel *v3high.Document\n24\t\t// ConvertedFrom is the original spec version when the source was not\n25\t\t// already OpenAPI 3.x — \"2.0\" for a converted Swagger document, empty\n26\t\t// otherwise. Commands that report on a spec can say so; everything else\n27\t\t// can ignore it, which is the point of converting at load time.\n28\t\tConvertedFrom string\n29\t}\n30\t"
      }
    ]
