uilder's buffer holding the resolved credential is left to the GC**\nSeverity: **INFO** · Blocked-by: none · `internal/curl/config.go:120-123`\n`config = []byte(doc.b.String())` makes a copy (`strings.Builder.String` returns a string aliasing `b.buf`; the `[]byte` conversion copies), and `doc.b.Reset()` sets `b.buf = nil` without zeroing it. So `cleanupWith` (config.go:382-389) zeroes the copy while the original heap buffer — containing the resolved bearer token or `user:password` — stays readable until the GC reuses that memory. The doc comment at config.go:85-87 claims the values \"do not linger in a buffer the rest of the process can still reach\", which is not what the code does. Outside strict concurrency scope, but it is on the same cleanup path.\nFix: zero `doc.b`'s bytes before `Reset()` — e.g. build into a `[]byte` field you own rather than a `strings.Builder`, and `clear()` it.\n\n**10. `storedIDs` treats any read failure as an empty store, allowing duplicate ids**\nSeverity: **INFO** · Blocked-by: none · `internal/corpus/store.go:161-163`\n`os.ReadFile` errors are all collapsed to `return nil`, not just `fs.ErrNotExist`. Every candidate id then looks free.\nFailure scenario: the history file's mode was changed to 0400 by an operator, or an EIO/ENOMEM on a large file. `uniqueID` returns the raw timestamp even though an entry already holds it; `write` appends the duplicate; `selectEntry` (history.go:419) resolves the id to the newest match, so `history replay <id>` silently re-issues a different request than the one shown — precisely the failure the comment at store.go:133-137 says the field exists to prevent.\nFix: distinguish `fs.ErrNotExist` from other errors and make a genuine read failure abort the append.",
