    "prompt": "You are the CONCURRENCY reviewer for a code review of the Go CLI project at /home/casper/projects/talaria, branch `ralph/design` vs base `main`.\n\nThe entire implementation is new on this branch. Scope: all of cmd/ and internal/. File list:\n  git -C /home/casper/projects/talaria diff --stat main...HEAD -- cmd internal\n\nRead docs/design/DESIGN.md §5a for the intended process/stdin model — it is load-bearing here:\n\"The Go process owns the real stdin. curl's stdin is always a fresh pipe that Go writes.\" `--body -` is read to EOF by Go; curl is spawned with a new pipe carrying the `-K -` config document.\n\nFocus areas, in priority order:\n1. internal/curl/exec.go, internal/curl/procgroup_unix.go, internal/curl/procgroup_other.go, internal/curl/sweep.go — subprocess lifecycle. Is every spawned curl guaranteed to be reaped? Can the writer goroutine feeding curl's stdin block forever if curl exits early (broken pipe)? Are stdout/stderr pipes fully drained before Wait()? Deadlock if curl writes more than the pipe buffer while Go is still writing the config document?\n2. Signal handling and context cancellation: cmd/talaria/root.go, cmd/talaria/call.go, cmd/talaria/run.go. Is SIGINT/SIGTERM handled and propagated? Is context cancellation honoured by the exec path and by the curl version preflight? Is the signal handler ever unregistered? Any goroutine leak per command invocation?\n3. cmd/talaria/run.go — if operations run concurrently, check for data races on shared result slices/maps, on the history/corpus store, and on any shared spec model. If it is sequential, say so and check timeout handling per operation instead.\n4. internal/corpus/store.go, entry.go, lock_unix.go, lock_other.go — cross-process file locking for the history store. Is the lock held for the whole read-modify-write? Is it released on every error path (defer)? Is the non-unix implementation a silent no-op, and does that corrupt data? Is the write atomic (temp file + rename) and is the temp file cleaned up on failure? Is there a TOCTOU between reading and writing?\n5. Errors swallowed rather than propagated anywhere in the above — a `_ =` on something that matters, or an error path that returns success.\n6. Any use of os.Exit that skips deferred cleanup (temp files holding secrets, locks).\n\nRULES:\n- Every finding MUST have a real `file.go:LINE` you verified by reading the file. No guesses. Read the actual code; do not reason from filenames.\n- CRIT = must not ship: data loss or silent corruption, a hang/deadlock, a crash, or a leaked secret. A leaked goroutine in a short-lived CLI process that exits immediately is usually WARN or INFO, not CRIT — judge by real impact. Do not inflate.\n- For each finding decide `Blocked-by: none` or `Blocked-by: design` (fix needs a policy the design doc never states — say which).\n- Only report code on this branch.\n\nReturn a numbered list: Title, Severity (CRIT/WARN/INFO), Blocked-by, File:line, Description, Suggested fix. Be rigorous and concise. Do not write any files.",
