        "type": "text",
        "text": "I read the actual sources in all five focus areas plus `os/exec`'s `Wait`/`awaitGoroutines` in the local Go 1.26 toolchain to confirm the subprocess semantics. Findings below.\n\n## Answers to the focus questions\n\n**1. Subprocess lifecycle (`internal/curl/`) — clean.** Every spawned curl is reaped: `ExecuteWith` uses `cmd.Run()`, which always calls `Wait()`. stdout/stderr are `bytes.Buffer` (non-`*os.File`), so `os/exec` drains them on dedicated goroutines concurrently with the stdin writer — no pipe-buffer deadlock is possible while Go is still writing the `-K -` document. The stdin writer cannot block forever: it gets EPIPE if curl exits early, and `cmd.WaitDelay = killGrace` (exec.go:92) plus an *always-set* `execCtx` deadline (exec.go:82) guarantees the WaitDelay timer eventually starts and `closeDescriptors` unblocks it. I confirmed in `/usr/local/go/src/os/exec/exec.go` `awaitGoroutines` that `Wait` never returns before the copy goroutines have finished, so `cleanupConfig()` zeroing the shared `config` backing array (exec.go:70-73) races with nothing. `isolate()` correctly overrides `CommandContext`'s default `Cancel` after it is set (exec.go:88 → 95) and SIGKILLs `-pgid`. This is well-built.\n\n**2. `cmd/talaria/run.go` is strictly sequential** (run.go:122-135, comment at 120-121). No goroutines exist anywhere in `cmd/` or `internal/` outside tests (`grep \"go func\"` → zero non-test hits), so there are no data races on the result slice, the shared `*gen.Generator`, the `*corpus.Store`, the `*validate.Validator` or the `*spec.Document`. Timeout is per-operation and non-cumulative (`r.opts` → fresh `execCtx` per call), which is correct. Cancellation mid-suite is handled and the in-flight result is correctly dropped before `append`.\n\n**3. The corpus lock is held for the whole read-modify-write** (store.go:109-126: `uniqueID` → `write` → `trim` all inside), released by `defer` on every path, and `replace()` is a proper temp-file+rename with a `defer os.Remove` cleanup. No TOCTOU on unix.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n**1. SIGINT/SIGTERM are trapped for the process lifetime but three blocking calls ignore the context, so the process becomes unkillable except by SIGKILL**\nSeverity: **WARN** · Blocked-by: none · `cmd/talaria/root.go:207`\n`signal.NotifyContext` registers a permanent `signal.Notify`; after the first signal the internal goroutine exits but the registration stays, so every subsequent SIGINT/SIGTERM is delivered to a buffered channel and silently discarded. Default termination is disabled for the whole run. That is fine as long as everything honours the context — but three reachable operations do not: `io.ReadAll(b.in.Stdin)` (`internal/request/body.go:102`), `syscall.Flock(..., LOCK_EX)` (`internal/corpus/lock_unix.go:36`), and the version preflight (`internal/curl/version.go:42`).\nFailure scenario: `talaria call --body - <op>` with stdin a terminal (or a pipe whose writer stalls). Go blocks in `ReadAll`. Ctrl-C does nothing; `kill -TERM <pid>` does nothing. Only Ctrl-D, Ctrl-\\ or `kill -9` gets out. In CI/systemd, a SIGTERM shutdown hangs until the SIGKILL timeout.\nFix: call `stop()` from a small watcher after the first signal so a second one restores default disposition — `go func(){ <-ctx.Done(); stop() }()` — and/or make `stdinBody` read under a context.\n\n**2. The curl version preflight spawns an unbounded, uncancellable subprocess**\nSeverity: **WARN** · Blocked-by: none · `internal/curl/version.go:42`\n`exec.Command(path, \"--version\").Output()` takes no context, no `WaitDelay`, and no process group. Every other curl in the tool is bounded by `execCtx` + `WaitDelay` + `Setpgid`; this one is not. It is reaped (`Output` → `Run` → `Wait`), so no zombie, but it is a hang.\nFailure scenario: `curl` on `PATH` is a wrapper script that blocks (an NFS/autofs stall, a shim waiting on a lock, a sandbox helper). `talaria call` hangs before it has done anything, and per finding 1 SIGTERM will not end it.\nFix: take a `context.Context` through `preflight(ctx, path)` (it is already called from `ExecuteWith`, which has one) and use `exec.CommandContext` with a short independent deadline, e.g. 5s.\n\n**3. Every recorded call reads and JSON-parses the entire history file twice while holding the exclusive lock**\nSeverity: **WARN** · Blocked-by: none · `internal/corpus/store.go:115` and `:126` (implementations at `:161` and `:251`)\n`Append` calls `uniqueID` → `storedIDs` → `os.ReadFile(path)` + `lineHead` per line, then `write` appends, then `trim` → `os.ReadFile(path)` again + `lineHead` per line. Both full scans happen under `flock(LOCK_EX)`. The store holds up to `maxPerSource`(1000) × 3 sources entries, each up to ~170 KB (`MaxBody` 64 KiB for request and response, base64-expanded).\nFailure scenario: `talaria run` over a 500-operation spec against a store that already holds a few thousand entries. Each of the 500 appends re-reads and re-unmarshals the whole file twice — quadratic. At a modest 4 KB/entry × 3000 entries that is ~24 MB read and 6000 unmarshals per operation, tens of seconds added to the suite; near the retention ceiling it is minutes per operation, i.e. indistinguishable from a hang, with the cross-process lock held the whole time.\nFix: `trim` only needs to run when the file could be over cap — check `os.Stat` size or a line count kept cheaply, or fold `storedIDs` and `trim`'s scan into a single pass and skip both entirely when the file is under a threshold.\n\n**4. The history lock blocks indefinitely with no deadline**\nSeverity: **WARN** · Blocked-by: design (the design doc states the lock must not give up — \"one that gave up would drop history the caller was told had been recorded\" — but never says what bounds the wait) · `internal/corpus/lock_unix.go:36`\n`LOCK_EX` without `LOCK_NB` and without any deadline. Go installs signal handlers with `SA_RESTART`, so the blocked `flock(2)` is not interruptible by SIGINT/SIGTERM either (compounding finding 1). Combined with finding 3, a long `run` holds the lock for a long time.\nFailure scenario: a `run` in one terminal holds the lock through a slow `trim`; `talaria call` in another terminal blocks in `flock` with no output and cannot be Ctrl-C'd. On a stale NFS mount holding the lock file, it blocks forever.\nFix: loop on `LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB` with a short sleep against a bounded deadline (a few seconds) and the caller's context, then fail with the existing \"cannot lock the history file\" error — `recordCall` already downgrades an Append failure to a stderr warning, so this loses nothing but a warning line.\n\n**5. The non-unix lock is a silent no-op, which permits interleaved lines and lost trims**\nSeverity: **INFO** · Blocked-by: none · `internal/corpus/lock_other.go:11`\nReturns `func(){}, nil` with no signal to the caller. On `!unix` (windows, plan9, wasip1) two concurrent talaria processes get no serialisation at all. `os.File.Write` loops over multiple `write(2)` calls for a large line (`internal/poll.FD.Write`), so two concurrent appends of a 170 KB entry can interleave into unparseable lines — `Read` skips them, so both entries vanish silently. Separately, `trim`'s rename (store.go:323) can drop an append that landed after its snapshot read, after `Append` already returned nil for it.\nImpact is limited because talaria targets unix, as the comment says.\nFix: either take a `golang.org/x/sys/windows` `LockFileEx` dependency, or make `New` refuse to record on a platform with no lock rather than recording unsafely.\n\n**6. `Append` reports failure for an entry that was successfully written**\nSeverity: **INFO** · Blocked-by: none · `internal/corpus/store.go:126`\n`write(path, ...)` has already durably appended the line when `trim(path)` runs; a `trim` failure (store.go:251 `os.ReadFile`, or `replace`'s `CreateTemp`/`Rename`) propagates as `Append`'s error.\nFailure scenario: the state directory is on a full filesystem. `write` succeeds (the line was small), `replace` fails on `CreateTemp`, and `recordCall` prints `warning: the call was not recorded in history: cannot stage the trimmed history: ...` — the exact opposite of what happened. An operator acting on that warning re-runs a mutating call believing nothing was recorded.\nFix: return a distinguishable error, or log the trim failure separately and return nil once the line is on disk.\n\n**7. `ErrWaitDelay` on a successful curl is reported as \"cannot run curl\"**\nSeverity: **INFO** · Blocked-by: none · `internal/curl/exec.go:104`, classified at `:119`\nWhen curl exits 0 but a grandchild still holds an output pipe, `Wait` returns `exec.ErrWaitDelay` after `killGrace`. Neither `ctx.Err()` nor `execCtx.Err()` is set, and `errors.As(err, &exitErr)` fails, so `runFailure` returns `clierr.RequestFailed(\"cannot run curl: exec: WaitDelay expired before I/O complete\")` — exit 1 for a request that actually completed. The response is in the capture files and is discarded.\nFailure scenario: `--proxy socks5h://` where the helper outlives curl, or any curl build that forks a helper inheriting stdout.\nFix: `if errors.Is(err, exec.ErrWaitDelay) && cmd.ProcessState.Success() { … }` fall through to `readResponse`.\n\n**8. Integer overflow on a large `--timeout` turns every request into an instant timeout**\nSeverity: **INFO** · Blocked-by: none · `internal/curl/exec.go:82`\n`context.WithTimeout(ctx, opts.MaxTime+killGrace)` overflows `int64` when `MaxTime > MaxInt64 - 2s`. `--timeout 9223372036` yields `MaxTime = 9223372036e9` ns, which fits, but `+2e9` wraps negative → an already-expired context.\nFailure scenario: `talaria call --timeout 9223372036 …` fails immediately with \"curl outlived its 2562047h47m16s timeout and was killed\". `withDefaults` (config.go:53) only guards the non-positive case, so nothing catches it.\nFix: clamp `MaxTime` to a sane ceiling in `withDefaults`, or saturate the addition.\n\n**9. `cleanupWith` zeroes only a copy of the config document; the builder's buffer holding the resolved credential is left to the GC**\nSeverity: **INFO** · Blocked-by: none · `internal/curl/config.go:120-123`\n`config = []byte(doc.b.String())` makes a copy (`strings.Builder.String` returns a string aliasing `b.buf`; the `[]byte` conversion copies), and `doc.b.Reset()` sets `b.buf = nil` without zeroing it. So `cleanupWith` (config.go:382-389) zeroes the copy while the original heap buffer — containing the resolved bearer token or `user:password` — stays readable until the GC reuses that memory. The doc comment at config.go:85-87 claims the values \"do not linger in a buffer the rest of the process can still reach\", which is not what the code does. Outside strict concurrency scope, but it is on the same cleanup path.\nFix: zero `doc.b`'s bytes before `Reset()` — e.g. build into a `[]byte` field you own rather than a `strings.Builder`, and `clear()` it.\n\n**10. `storedIDs` treats any read failure as an empty store, allowing duplicate ids**\nSeverity: **INFO** · Blocked-by: none · `internal/corpus/store.go:161-163`\n`os.ReadFile` errors are all collapsed to `return nil`, not just `fs.ErrNotExist`. Every candidate id then looks free.\nFailure scenario: the history file's mode was changed to 0400 by an operator, or an EIO/ENOMEM on a large file. `uniqueID` returns the raw timestamp even though an entry already holds it; `write` appends the duplicate; `selectEntry` (history.go:419) resolves the id to the newest match, so `history replay <id>` silently re-issues a different request than the one shown — precisely the failure the comment at store.go:133-137 says the field exists to prevent.\nFix: distinguish `fs.ErrNotExist` from other errors and make a genuine read failure abort the append."
      }
    ],
