        "text": "## Spec-compliance review — talaria `ralph/design` vs DESIGN.md v0.4\n\nVerified against a built binary (`go build ./cmd/talaria`) with live reproductions where noted. Full test suite passes (`go test ./...` — all green), so none of the below is caught by existing tests. No Phase 5–8 code exists (no `internal/twin`, no recording proxy) — no scope creep.\n\nPer your instruction I did **not** re-verify or report amendment (a) (`--allow-host` / `allow_hosts` / `credentials_withheld`).\n\n---\n\n### 1. `history replay` sends live credentials to the host named by the stored entry\n**Severity: CRIT · Blocked-by: none**\n`/home/casper/projects/talaria/cmd/talaria/history.go:578`\n\n`replayRequest` sets `BaseURL: parsed.Scheme + \"://\" + parsed.Host` from `entry.URL`. DESIGN §5a \"History is untrusted input when read\", Target-host row: *\"From the current `--base-url`, profile, or spec — never from the stored URL. If the stored host is outside the currently allowed set, replay refuses with exit 2 rather than silently retargeting.\"*\n\n**Failure scenario (reproduced):** a `history.jsonl` line with `\"url\":\"http://127.0.0.1:9999/steal\"` and `\"Authorization\":\"Bearer <redacted:env:TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER>\"`, replayed with `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER=SUPER-SECRET-TOKEN`, produced on the listener:\n```\nGET /steal HTTP/1.1\nHost: 127.0.0.1:9999\nAuthorization: Bearer SUPER-SECRET-TOKEN\n```\nExit 0. This is exactly the review finding Amendment 2 was written to close, still open.\n\n**Fix:** derive `BaseURL` from `--base-url` → profile → spec (replay must load a spec), and refuse with exit 2 when the stored host is not in the resolved allowed set. Shares infrastructure with (a) but the \"never from the stored URL\" half is implementable independently.\n\n---\n\n### 2. `history replay` never re-derives the request; it replays stored fields verbatim and loads no spec\n**Severity: CRIT · Blocked-by: none**\n`/home/casper/projects/talaria/cmd/talaria/history.go:163` (command), `:554`–`:609` (`replayRequest`)\n\nDESIGN §5a row 1: *\"operationId, params, body — Re-bound through the normal request-construction path, re-validated against the current spec.\"* `newHistoryReplayCmd` never calls `loadSpec`; `replayRequest` copies `entry.Method`, `parsed.EscapedPath()`, stored headers/cookies/body straight onto a `request.Request` without `request.Build`, without `index.Lookup`, and without `config.Resolve`. The code comment at `history.go:230` states the omission of validation as intentional (\"replay reads a recorded request and needs no spec\"), which is now design drift.\n\n**Failure scenario:** a hand-edited or foreign entry can make talaria issue an arbitrary method + path + header set + body to an arbitrary host (finding 1 shows the host half), none of it constrained by the spec. Independently: an entry whose operation was removed or whose required param changed replays against the old contract with no validation block and no error.\n\n**Fix:** replay should re-resolve the operation by `entry.OperationID` against the current spec, re-bind params/body through `request.Build`, re-resolve credentials via `config.Resolve`, and emit a `validation` block.\n\n---\n\n### 3. `auth check` reports nothing and exits 0 for a spec whose only scheme is oauth2 — while `call` refuses it\n**Severity: CRIT · Blocked-by: none**\n`/home/casper/projects/talaria/internal/config/auth.go:184` (`Schemes` filters out any scheme where `schemeReason() != \"\"`), `/home/casper/projects/talaria/cmd/talaria/auth.go:183` (`satisfied` skips `config.Unsupported`), `/home/casper/projects/talaria/cmd/talaria/auth.go:24` (`authScheme` has no `supported` field)\n\nDESIGN §5 as amended: *\"`auth check` reports `{\"scheme\":\"oauth2\",\"supported\":false,\"present\":false}` and exits 5\"* and *\"`auth check` never reports a scheme satisfied when the call would refuse it. The two agree by construction, or `auth check` is worthless to an agent.\"*\n\n**Reproduced** against a spec declaring only `oauth2`:\n```\n$ talaria auth check /tmp/oauth.yaml --output json\n{\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\",\"schemes\":[]}     exit=0\n$ talaria call /tmp/oauth.yaml listPets --dry-run\n{\"...\",\"error\":{\"code\":2,\"message\":\"no usable security scheme for listPets: scheme oauth2 is of unsupported type oauth2\"}}   exit=2\n```\nThe same holds for an `apiKey` in an unsupported `in` (`schemeReason` returns a reason → dropped from the report).\n\n**Fix:** `Schemes` must return unsupported schemes too, carrying `supported:false`; `authScheme` needs the `supported` field; `satisfied` must count `Unsupported` as blocking unless `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER` is set.\n\n---\n\n### 4. `call` exits 2 and `run` exits 0-with-skip for an unsupported scheme; `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER` does not satisfy it\n**Severity: CRIT · Blocked-by: none**\n`/home/casper/projects/talaria/internal/config/auth.go:167` (`clierr.Usage` → exit 2), `/home/casper/projects/talaria/cmd/talaria/run.go:354`–`357` (`config.Resolve` error becomes `res.skip(...)`)\n\nDESIGN §5: *\"if `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER` is set, the scheme is satisfied by that token … if it is not set … `call` and `run` exit 5 with a structured error naming the scheme and the variable to set.\"* §4's exit table makes 5 the code an agent branches on to ask a human for a variable.\n\n**Reproduced:** with `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER=tok` set, `call` on the oauth2 spec still exits **2** with \"no usable security scheme\"; `run` reports `outcome:\"skipped\"` and exits **0** — a green smoke run over an API that was never tested. Neither message names a variable to export.\n\n**Fix:** in `config.Resolve`, satisfy an `Unsupported` requirement with `secret.Env(EnvBearer)` when present; otherwise return `clierr.CredentialMissing` naming the scheme and `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER`. In `run.go`, route a `CodeCredentialMissing` from `Resolve` through `noteMissing` rather than into `skip`.\n\n---\n\n### 5. `--output tsv` emits structurally invalid rows — spec text containing tab or newline is not escaped\n**Severity: CRIT · Blocked-by: none**\n`/home/casper/projects/talaria/internal/output/render.go:105`–`114`\n\n`tsvRenderer.Render` does `strings.Join(row, \"\\t\")` with no escaping. Cell values are spec-derived free text (`op.Summary` at `cmd/talaria/list.go:103`, `r.Summary` at `search.go:66`, `res.Reason` at `run.go:587`, recorded URLs and bodies at `history.go:288`).\n\n**Reproduced** — one operation whose summary is `\"line one\\nline two\\twith tab\"`:\n```\nGET^I/pets^IlistPets^Iline one$\nline two^Iwith tab$\nGET^I/dogs^IlistDogs^Iplain$\n```\nTwo operations become three rows with differing column counts; `cut -f3` silently returns garbage. README:\"`tsv` prints bare tab-separated rows … for `cut` and `awk`\" is not honoured.\n\n**Fix:** escape or strip `\\t`/`\\r`/`\\n` in TSV cells (and in pretty cells, which tabwriter also mis-aligns).\n\n---\n\n### 6. A spec with a large `minLength` crashes the process with an out-of-memory panic\n**Severity: CRIT · Blocked-by: none**\n`/home/casper/projects/talaria/internal/gen/gen.go:314`\n\n`clampLength` does `s += strings.Repeat(\"x\", int(*schema.MinLength)-len(s))` with no bound. The spec is untrusted input by the tool's own doctrine (README: *\"a spec talaria was pointed at is untrusted input\"*), and DESIGN §1 sells pointing it at any API's doc, including a fetched URL.\n\n**Reproduced:** a parameter schema with `minLength: 9007199254740991` in a `talaria run` panics:\n```\nruntime: out of memory\nstrings.Repeat(...) /usr/local/go/src/strings/strings.go:677\ngen.clampLength /home/casper/projects/talaria/internal/gen/gen.go:314\n```\nProcess death, no structured error, no exit code an agent can branch on. This is cycle-2 finding 7, listed in the amendments doc as \"already specified — implementation miss\", still open.\n\n**Fix:** cap the pad at a sane ceiling (e.g. `MaxBody` or a few KiB) and report a structured skip reason when the schema cannot be satisfied within it.\n\n---\n\n### 7. `history replay` silently ignores `--base-url` and the profile's base URL\n**Severity: WARN · Blocked-by: none**\n`/home/casper/projects/talaria/cmd/talaria/history.go:175`–`233`\n\n`--base-url` is a persistent root flag (`root.go:73`) so cobra accepts it, but the replay `RunE` never reads it. **Reproduced:** `talaria history replay evil --base-url https://elsewhere.example.com` still went to `127.0.0.1:9999`. A flag that is accepted and discarded is worse than one that errors — the caller believes they retargeted the request. DESIGN §5a names `--base-url` as the *first* source of the replay target.\n\n**Fix:** falls out of fixing finding 1; until then, at minimum reject `--base-url` on `replay` with exit 2.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Server variables are never substituted, so a templated `servers[0].url` makes a spec uncallable\n**Severity: WARN · Blocked-by: none**\n`/home/casper/projects/talaria/internal/request/build.go:173`–`179` (`firstServer` returns `Servers[0].URL` raw), rejected at `:158`–`:162`\n\nDESIGN §5a: *\"every host in the spec's `servers[]`, **after server-variable substitution**\"*. `Server.Variables` is never read anywhere in the tree (`grep -rn \"Variables\"` over `cmd/ internal/` returns only unrelated comments).\n\n**Reproduced:** a spec with `url: \"https://{region}.api.example.com/v1\"` and `variables.region.default: eu`:\n```\n{\"error\":{\"code\":2,\"message\":\"cannot build a request for listPets: base URL \\\"https://{region}.api.example.com/v1\\\" from the spec's servers[0].url is not an absolute http(s) URL\"}}  exit=2\n```\nFails loudly rather than silently, hence WARN — but it makes a large class of real specs unusable without `--base-url`, and Amendment 1's host set is meaningless until it is fixed (the amendments doc says \"Fix 5 first\").\n\n**Fix:** substitute each variable's `default` (and validate against `enum`) in `firstServer`, and expose the substituted set for the future host-binding check.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Parameter-level and media-type-level `example`/`examples` are ignored by `run`'s data chain\n**Severity: WARN · Blocked-by: none**\n`/home/casper/projects/talaria/internal/operation/operation.go:49`–`60` (`Param` carries no `Example`/`Examples`), `/home/casper/projects/talaria/internal/gen/fixtures.go:172`–`186` (`paramValue` only consults `p.Schema.Schema()`'s example), `:190`–`:216` (`bodyFor` likewise; `operation.MediaType` at `operation.go:80`–`83` has no example field)\n\nDESIGN §5a: *\"Priority order: spec `example`/`examples` → user fixture files → schema-generated data.\"* Only *schema*-level examples participate. OpenAPI puts `example`/`examples` on the Parameter Object and the Media Type Object as well, and that is where spec authors usually write them.\n\n**Reproduced:** a path parameter with `example: 42` at the parameter level; `talaria run --base-url http://127.0.0.1:9998` sent `GET /pets/foxtrot-906` — generated data, not the documented example. `describe --output json` also omits it entirely.\n\n**Fix:** carry `Example`/`Examples` on `operation.Param` and `operation.MediaType`, and consult them ahead of the schema's in `paramValue`/`bodyFor`.\n\n---\n\n### 10. History entries are not size-bounded on read; `MaxBody` applies only at write\n**Severity: WARN · Blocked-by: none**\n`/home/casper/projects/talaria/internal/corpus/store.go:187` (unbounded `os.ReadFile`), `/home/casper/projects/talaria/internal/corpus/entry.go:121`–`135` (`Body.Bytes()` base64-decodes with no cap), `entry.go:254` (`newBody` caps only on the write path)\n\nDESIGN §5a, Sizes-and-types row: *\"Bounded and type-checked before use. A corrupt or hostile entry fails that entry, never the process.\"* Type-checking is handled (a line that will not unmarshal is skipped at `store.go:202`), but there is no size bound anywhere on the read path. A hand-edited or copied `history.jsonl` with a multi-hundred-megabyte `data` field is read whole into memory by every `history`, `history show` and `history replay` invocation, and `Bytes()` decodes it again — process-level failure rather than entry-level.\n\n**Fix:** bound the file read and reject any single line or decoded body over `MaxBody` as a malformed entry.\n\n---\n\n### 11. Replay's env-name allowlist admits names outside the `TALARIA_AUTH_*` namespace\n**Severity: WARN · Blocked-by: design**\n`/home/casper/projects/talaria/cmd/talaria/history.go:733` (`return prof.ReferencesEnv(ref.Name)`), `/home/casper/projects/talaria/internal/config/auth.go:328`–`340`\n\nDESIGN §5a is unconditional: *\"Only names inside the `TALARIA_AUTH_*` namespace are resolvable. A stored entry naming any other variable is malformed, not a lookup.\"* The code additionally resolves any variable the active profile's `auth:` map references (e.g. `${STAGING_TOKEN}`) — which is what AGENT.md:249–251 and README:263–268 document.\n\n**Failure scenario:** with `--profile staging` whose `auth: {bearerAuth: ${STAGING_TOKEN}}`, a stored entry naming `env:STAGING_TOKEN` in an arbitrary header position resolves and is transmitted — permitted by AGENT.md, forbidden by DESIGN §5a as written.\n\n**Missing decision:** whether the replay allowlist is strictly `TALARIA_AUTH_*` (DESIGN v0.4) or `TALARIA_AUTH_*` **plus** the active profile's referenced variables (AGENT.md/README/code). The code's behaviour is defensible — a profile is human-authored and only in force when `--profile` is passed — but two shipped documents now disagree, and the reviewer cannot pick.\n\n**Fix:** amend §5a's row to name the profile's variables, or narrow `replayableEnv` and AGENT.md/README to the namespace.\n\n---\n\n### 12. README documents the pre-amendment auth behaviour as intended\n**Severity: WARN · Blocked-by: none**\n`/home/casper/projects/talaria/README.md:254`, and `README.md:165`–`170`\n\n> \"schemes talaria cannot supply at all (OAuth2, OpenID Connect) are left out rather than reported missing.\"\n\nThat is the exact behaviour DESIGN §5 v0.4 now forbids. README:165–170's \"bring your own token and let a `bearer` scheme carry it\" also does not describe a reachable workaround: a spec that declares only `oauth2` has no bearer scheme for a token to be carried by, so `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER` is inert (verified in finding 3).\n\n**Fix:** rewrite both passages alongside the fix for findings 3 and 4. (AGENT.md is silent on unsupported schemes, so it needs the new behaviour added rather than corrected.)\n\n---\n\n## Verified as compliant\n\nChecked and found to match DESIGN.md / AGENT.md / README, in case it narrows follow-up:\n\n- **§4 command surface:** `list --tag`, `describe [--depth]`, `search --kind`, `uses`, `call` (all ten flags), `history [--operation|--since|--status|--source]`, `history show|replay`, `run` (all seven flags incl. `--fixtures`), `auth check [--profile]`, `version` — all present with documented behaviour.\n- **§4 exit codes:** 0 (4xx is exit 0), 1 (`curl/exec.go:257`, cancellation), 2 (usage, incl. group-without-subcommand via `root.go:129`), 3 (`spec/load.go`, `spec/convert.go`), 4 (`call.go:425`, `run.go:451`), 5 for *supported* schemes (`secret/secret.go:131`, `auth.go:155`, `run.go:447`) — all reproduced.\n- **Output contract:** `\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\"` spliced as the first key (`output/render.go:73`), `call` envelope matches §4's sketch field-for-field, `validation` block carries the three independent booleans plus per-field `errors`, stderr errors are one line of the same envelope with `valid_alternatives` (`clierr.go:117`–`126`).\n- **§5a leak channels:** symbolic curl (`curl/render.go`), `<redacted:env:NAME>` in `request.headers`, `-q -K -` config-on-stdin with never-argv secrets (`curl/config.go:109`), 0600 temp-file fallback for large bodies, built-in header globs incl. `Set-Cookie` (`secret/redact.go:19`–`27`), built-in response body paths `access_token`/`refresh_token`/`id_token` (`secret/response.go:17`), write-time history redaction, errors built from the redacted representation, no `--verbose`, one-shot query-key stderr warning (reproduced). `internal/canary` implements the required CI leak suite.\n- **§5a test-data order for `run`:** schema example → fixture → gen, applied per field (`gen/fixtures.go:127`–`161`) — correct as far as it goes; see finding 9 for the missing example locations.\n- **§3 principles:** no prompts, no pager, no interactive code paths (`os.Stdin` appears only as the injected `--body -` reader); pretty-on-TTY / JSON-when-piped with `--output` winning (`output/output.go:64`); curl ≥ 7.70 floor enforced once per process (`curl/version.go:15`, `:77`); `--dry-run` prints the identical curl, sends nothing, records nothing, and exits 0 (reproduced).\n- **§5 boundary rules:** `operation`/`validate`/`gen` import neither `curl` nor `twin`; `secret` resolves only in `internal/curl` at exec time."
      }
