            "text": "INTEGRATION review — data actually traced end to end, with a built binary + real curl + a local recording server. All findings below were verified by reading the file and, where marked *verified empirically*, by running the binary.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Resolved credentials are sent to any host `--base-url` names; §5a \"Credentials bind to hosts\" is entirely unimplemented\n**Severity: CRIT · Blocked-by: none**\n**File: internal/request/build.go:498** (also build.go:134, cmd/talaria/call.go:292)\n\n`binder.credentials` attaches every resolved `config.Credential` to the request unconditionally. There is no host check anywhere: `grep -rn \"credentials_withheld\\|allow-host\\|allow_hosts\"` over all `.go` returns **zero hits** — only DESIGN.md and the amendment proposal mention them. `config.Profile` (internal/config/config.go:56) has no `allow_hosts` field, and `Config` uses `KnownFields(true)`, so adding one to a profile is a parse error today.\n\n*Verified empirically:* with `servers[0].url = https://api.example.com` and `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER=SUPERSECRET`, `talaria call spec.yaml listPets --base-url http://127.0.0.1:8765` produced `AUTH=Bearer SUPERSECRET` at the off-spec listener, exit 0, with no stderr warning and no `credentials_withheld` field in the envelope.\n\nDESIGN.md §5a states this as a v0.4 invariant: *\"a resolved credential is transmitted only to a host the spec declares, or one a human has explicitly allowed\"*, with an exact machine-readable output shape. §5a also names the exact attack: *\"without that second rule, `--base-url https://attacker.example` sends a production key to an attacker while violating nothing.\"* Nothing else in the code substitutes for it — redaction answers \"does it print\", not \"who receives it\".\n\n**Fix:** compute the allowed host set in one place (spec `servers[]` after variable substitution ∪ `--allow-host` ∪ profile `allow_hosts`), pass it into `request.Build`, and have `credentials` drop each out-of-set credential into a `[]Withheld` on the `Request` that `callView`/`runResult` render as `credentials_withheld` plus a one-line stderr warning. `auth check` must report against the same resolved set.\n\n---\n\n### 2. `history replay` takes its target host from the stored entry — a hand-edited history file exfiltrates the live credential\n**Severity: CRIT · Blocked-by: none**\n**File: cmd/talaria/history.go:578**\n\n```go\nBaseURL:     parsed.Scheme + \"://\" + parsed.Host,\n```\n\nThe base URL is reconstructed from `entry.URL`, i.e. from the JSONL file. `--base-url` and the profile's base URL are never consulted on this path. `replayableEnv` (history.go:721) restricts *which* variable may be resolved, but nothing restricts *where the resolved value goes*.\n\n*Verified empirically:* I recorded a real call, edited `history.jsonl` to change `\"url\"` to `http://127.0.0.1:8766/steal`, then ran `talaria history replay 1`. The listener logged `AUTH=Bearer SUPERSECRET`, exit 0.\n\nDESIGN.md §5a's replay table is explicit: *\"Target host | From the current `--base-url`, profile, or spec — never from the stored URL. If the stored host is outside the currently allowed set, replay refuses with exit 2 rather than silently retargeting.\"* README.md:397 even names this exact threat (\"send it to a host of the file's choosing\") and then only closes the variable-name half of it.\n\n**Fix:** derive the base URL for replay from `--base-url` / profile / spec as `call` does; compare `parsed.Host` against the allowed set from finding 1 and refuse with exit 2 when it is outside.\n\n---\n\n### 3. `auth check` reports nothing and exits 0 for a scheme `call` refuses with exit 2, and `run` exits 0 with everything skipped\n**Severity: CRIT · Blocked-by: none**\n**Files: internal/config/auth.go:184, cmd/talaria/auth.go:188, internal/config/auth.go:167, cmd/talaria/run.go:356**\n\n`config.Schemes` filters out any scheme `schemeReason` rejects (auth.go:184), so an oauth2/openIdConnect scheme never appears in the report. `auth.satisfied` returns `!usable` (auth.go:188), and `Unsupported` never sets `usable`, so such an operation counts as satisfied. `config.Resolve` meanwhile returns `clierr.Usage` (auth.go:167) → **exit 2**. `runner.execute` turns the same error into a *skip* (run.go:356) without adding to `r.missing`, so the suite exits 0.\n\n*Verified empirically* on a spec whose only scheme is `oauth2`, with `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER=TOK` exported:\n```\nauth check → {\"schema\":\"talaria/v1\",\"schemes\":[]}                     exit 0\ncall       → error code 2 \"no usable security scheme for listPets\"    exit 2\nrun        → 1 skipped, 0 passed                                       exit 0\n```\n\nThree DESIGN.md §5 statements broken at once: the required report object `{\"scheme\":\"oauth2\",\"supported\":false,\"present\":false}`; *\"`auth check` … exits 5; `call` and `run` exit 5\"*; and the bring-your-own-token rule *\"if `TALARIA_AUTH_BEARER` is set, the scheme is satisfied by that token\"* (it is set and is ignored). Most seriously it violates the rule stated as non-negotiable: *\"`auth check` never reports a scheme satisfied when the call would refuse it. The two agree by construction, or `auth check` is worthless to an agent.\"* The `run` case is also the green-CI-that-proves-nothing outcome that `selectOperations` (run.go:198) explicitly refuses to allow elsewhere. Note README.md:254 documents the *current* behaviour, so the docs conflict too.\n\n**Fix:** add `Supported bool` to `config.Credential`/`authScheme`, have `Schemes` emit unsupported schemes with `supported:false, present:false`, satisfy them from `EnvBearer` when it is set, and make the unsatisfiable case `clierr.CredentialMissing` (5) in `Resolve`, `auth check` and `run`'s verdict.\n\n---\n\n### 4. `history replay` silently sends the redacted request body\n**Severity: WARN · Blocked-by: none**\n**File: cmd/talaria/history.go:595**\n\n`corpus.Redactors.requestBody` (internal/corpus/entry.go:242) applies the response body-path redactor to the *request* body before storing, so `{\"refresh_token\":\"REAL\"}` is stored as `{\"refresh_token\":\"<redacted>\"}` — correct per §5a. But `replayRequest` only checks `body.Truncated`; it never scans the decoded body for `secret.Placeholder`, so it sends the redacted document verbatim. Headers, cookies and query params get `warnUnreplayable` (history.go:746) for exactly this situation; the body gets nothing.\n\n*Verified empirically:* the replayed POST arrived at the server as `{\"grant\":\"x\",\"refresh_token\":\"\\u003credacted\\u003e\"}` with an empty stderr.\n\n**Fix:** scan the decoded body for `secret.Placeholder` and either refuse the replay (as with a truncated body) or warn on stderr the way `warnUnreplayable` does.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Canary suite has no validation/`--fail-on-error` error-path case, and its own comment saying why is stale\n**Severity: WARN · Blocked-by: none**\n**File: internal/canary/canary_test.go:427**\n\n```go\n// A response-validation failure is not here because response validation is\n// not built yet (plan tasks 26 and 27). Whoever adds --fail-on-error adds\n// the case; the stages that exist are all covered.\n```\n\nResponse validation *is* built on this branch (`internal/validate`, `call --fail-on-error` at cmd/talaria/call.go:215, exit 4). §5a's leak-channel table names *\"validation errors quoting the request\"* as a channel and adds *\"Test explicitly — error paths are where redaction bugs live.\"* The `stages` table (canary_test.go:430–480) has no exit-4 stage, so nothing greps the `validation.errors[]` messages, the `callFailure` stderr, or the `run --report junit` `<failure message=…>` for a canary.\n\n**Fix:** add a stage that calls an operation returning a schema-violating body with a credential set, asserting exit 4, and a `run --report junit --fail-on-error` counterpart; scan both streams and the junit XML.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Canary suite never injects a credential through a profile `auth:` reference — only through env-var convention\n**Severity: WARN · Blocked-by: none**\n**File: internal/canary/canary_test.go:655**\n\nEvery entry in `mechanisms` (canary_test.go:99–151) sets a `TALARIA_AUTH_*` variable. The only profile-`auth:` case is `TestACredentialResolutionFailureNamesNoValue`, which tests a *rejected literal*. The successful path — `profileRef` at internal/config/auth.go:348 resolving `${STAGING_TOKEN}` — is never carried through a real call by the canary suite, and DESIGN.md §5 names profiles as one of the two credential sources (\"A CI suite injects canary secrets through **every** auth mechanism\"). This is also the path that widens `replayableEnv` (history.go:733) beyond the `TALARIA_AUTH_*` namespace, so it is the higher-risk of the two.\n\n**Fix:** add a mechanism whose `env` sets an arbitrary variable name and whose harness writes `profiles: {p: {auth: {bearerAuth: \"${MY_TOKEN}\"}}}`, driven through the same `call`/`history`/`replay`/`run` sequence.\n\n---\n\n### 7. The one converted Swagger 2.0 `body` parameter is never executed end to end\n**Severity: WARN · Blocked-by: none**\n**File: internal/e2e/e2e_test.go:914**\n\n`TestASwagger2SpecFlowsThroughTheWholeLoop` asserts `\"createPet\": \"skipped\"` — it is a POST and `--allow-mutations` is not passed on the 2.0 path, though it *is* exercised on the 3.0 spec (e2e_test.go:728). So the 2.0 `in: body` → `requestBody` conversion, and the `consumes:`→content-type mapping, are never sent on the wire. `internal/spec/convert_test.go` asserts operation ids, servers, security schemes and `$ref`s, but nothing about `consumes`/`produces`/body parameters. DESIGN.md §5 and §7 both say *\"Phase 1 must verify conversion fidelity against real 2.0 specs.\"* `run.go:523`'s `headersFor` comment explicitly worries about \"a converted Swagger 2.0 formData operation\" with nothing covering it.\n\nI confirmed manually that the conversion is in fact correct (`talaria describe e2e-api-2.0.json createPet` yields `request_body.content[0].content_type = application/json` with the right required fields) — so this is a coverage gap, not a live defect.\n\n**Fix:** run `createPet` on `spec2Path` with `--allow-mutations` and assert the server saw a JSON body under `Content-Type: application/json`; add a convert_test asserting `consumes`/`produces` land on `requestBody.content` / `responses.*.content`.\n\n---\n\n### 8. The remote spec cache never expires and cannot be bypassed\n**Severity: WARN · Blocked-by: design** (DESIGN.md §4 says only \"URL (with local cache)\"; no TTL, invalidation or refresh policy is ever stated)\n**File: internal/spec/source.go:86**\n\n```go\nif data, err := os.ReadFile(cachePath); err == nil {\n    return loadBytes(data, url)\n}\n```\n\nOnce a URL-sourced spec is cached under `sha256(url)` it is served forever. There is no TTL, no conditional request, no `--refresh`/`--no-cache` flag, and no command that clears it. Every downstream stage — `list`, `describe`, request binding, and especially `validate` — then works against a contract that may be months stale, and `run` will report schema violations the server never committed, with no signal distinguishing that from a real one.\n\n**Fix:** decide a policy (a TTL, or an `ETag`/`Last-Modified` conditional GET, plus a `--refresh` escape hatch) and record it in DESIGN.md §4.\n\n---\n\n### 9. `internal/corpus` imports `internal/curl`, which will pull the executor into the twin's serve side\n**Severity: INFO · Blocked-by: none**\n**File: internal/corpus/entry.go:24**\n\n`NewEntry` takes a `*curl.Response`, so `corpus` depends on the outbound executor. DESIGN.md §5 says *\"`corpus` backs both `history` and the twin\"* and *\"Twin's serve side uses `net/http` directly — curl is only for outbound calls.\"* `internal/e2e/boundary_test.go:33` guards `operation`/`validate`/`gen` against `curl`/`corpus`/`twin` but says nothing about `corpus → curl`, so this passes today and will silently drag the executor into the twin when Phase 6 lands.\n\n**Fix:** have `NewEntry` take a small local `Observed{Status, Headers, Body, TimingMS}` struct that `cmd/talaria` fills from `curl.Response`, and add `corpus` to the shared list in `boundary_test.go`.\n\n---\n\n### 10. `history` list drops `timing_ms` for a call that rounded to 0ms\n**Severity: INFO · Blocked-by: none**\n**File: cmd/talaria/history.go:48**\n\n`TimingMS int64 \\`json:\"timing_ms,omitempty\"\\`` — a call against a local twin or `127.0.0.1` that rounds to 0ms loses the field entirely, and an agent cannot tell \"0ms\" from \"no response observed\". `run` uses `*int64` at cmd/talaria/run.go:57 for precisely this reason, with a comment saying so. The stored entry (`corpus.EntryResponse.TimingMS`, entry.go:92) has no `omitempty` and is correct; the loss is only in the `history` list view.\n\n**Fix:** make `historyEntryView.TimingMS` a `*int64` set only when `entry.Response != nil`, matching `runResult`.\n\n---\n\n**What does flow correctly** (traced and confirmed, so it is not re-litigated): spec → operation → request → curl config → exec → parse → validate → output → corpus for the 3.0 and 2.0 GET paths; `--write-out '%{json}'` `time_total` → `Response.TimingMS` → `runResult.TimingMS` → `Case.Seconds` → JUnit `time` attr with correct rounding; `--dry-run`'s emitted curl proven identical to the executed request by replaying it against the same server (e2e_test.go:480); Swagger 2.0 security schemes, `host`+`basePath`+`schemes`, `$ref`s, and body/consumes conversion; `history` write→`show`→`replay` id/index round trip; no test-only branches, hardcoded paths or test-only env vars in non-test code; the canary and e2e suites build and drive the **real binary with real curl** and defeat Go's test cache via `readSources()`; CI runs `go test ./...` and `internal/ci/workflow_test.go` pins that against `.ralph/stack.json`, so the canary suite genuinely gates the build."
