    "prompt": "You are the INTEGRATION reviewer for a code review of the Go CLI project at /home/casper/projects/talaria, branch `ralph/design` vs base `main`.\n\nThe entire implementation is new on this branch. Scope: all of cmd/ and internal/, plus .github/workflows/ci.yml. File list:\n  git -C /home/casper/projects/talaria diff --stat main...HEAD\n\nRead docs/design/DESIGN.md (v0.4) for the intended component boundaries (§5 \"Key boundary rules\") and the pipeline. Read AGENT.md and README.md too — they document the agent-facing workflow that must actually work end to end.\n\nThe system is a multi-stage pipeline. Your job is to VERIFY DATA ACTUALLY FLOWS between stages, not to assume it:\n1. spec load (internal/spec) -> operation model (internal/operation) -> request build (internal/request) -> curl config/argv (internal/curl) -> exec -> parse response -> validate (internal/validate) -> output (internal/output) -> history append (internal/corpus). Trace at least one real path end to end and find where a field produced upstream is dropped, renamed, or silently defaulted downstream.\n2. Swagger 2.0 -> 3.x conversion (internal/spec/convert.go): does everything downstream actually receive the converted model? Do security schemes, consumes/produces, body params survive conversion?\n3. `history` write -> `history show` -> `history replay` round trip. Does what is written actually round-trip back? Does replay reconstruct a request equivalent to the original?\n4. `run` (cmd/talaria/run.go) -> report renderers (internal/output/junit.go, render.go). Do timing, status, and validation results produced by the executor actually reach the JUnit and JSON reports with correct values? DESIGN.md says timings come from curl `--write-out`.\n5. Environment parity: does anything behave differently under `go test` than at runtime? Look for test-only branches, hardcoded paths, TTY detection that flips behaviour, env vars set only in tests, or tests that stub the curl executor so thoroughly they would not catch a real breakage.\n6. The canary CI suite (internal/canary/, internal/ci/workflow_test.go, .github/workflows/ci.yml): DESIGN.md §5a mandates a suite that injects canary secrets through EVERY auth mechanism and greps EVERY output surface (json, pretty, tsv, dry-run, errors, junit reports, history store, corpus files, debug logs). Verify the suite actually covers every mechanism and every surface named, and that CI actually runs it and fails the build on a leak. Gaps here are real findings.\n7. internal/e2e/: do the end-to-end tests exercise the real binary and real curl, or do they shortcut?\n\nRULES:\n- Every finding MUST have a real `file.go:LINE` (or ci.yml:LINE) you verified by reading the file. No guesses.\n- CRIT = must not ship: silently wrong results the caller cannot detect, data loss, a broken contract DESIGN.md states explicitly, or a leaked secret. Missing test coverage is normally WARN. Do not inflate.\n- For each finding decide `Blocked-by: none` or `Blocked-by: design` (fix requires a policy decision the doc never makes — state it).\n- Only report code on this branch.\n\nReturn a numbered list: Title, Severity (CRIT/WARN/INFO), Blocked-by, File:line, Description, Suggested fix. Be rigorous and concise. Do not write any files.",
